// Normative rule hcounteren_acc (src/priv/hypervisor.adoc):
//   In addition, when the TM bit in the hcounteren register is clear, attempts to access the
//     vstimecmp register (via stimecmp) while executing in VS-mode will cause a virtual-instruction
//     exception if the same bit in mcounteren is set. When this bit and the same bit in mcounteren
//     are both set, access to the vstimecmp register (if implemented) is permitted in VS-mode.
//
// Context:
//   menvcfg.STCE=1 and henvcfg.STCE=1 throughout. hcounteren.TM does not apply to HS-mode.
//
// Clarifications:
//   https://github.com/riscv/riscv-isa-manual/issues/2040: VS-mode reaches vstimecmp only through
//     the stimecmp address; hcounteren.TM gates that access, and vstimecmp's own address is not
//     accessible from V=1 (radimkrcmar: "vstimecmp CSR access in VS mode is not affected by the TM
//     bit").
//   https://github.com/riscv/riscv-isa-manual/issues/1898: with mcounteren.TM=0, a VS-mode stimecmp
//     access raises illegal-instruction, not virtual-instruction: the virtual-instruction rule
//     covers instructions disabled by a supervisor or hypervisor CSR, "which clearly does not apply
//     to an instruction being disabled by a machine CSR such as mcounteren" (gfavor).
//   hcounteren bits may be read-only zero (hcounteren_warl). Tests set hcounteren.TM, read it back,
//     and where it reads 0 expect VS-mode accesses to raise virtual-instruction instead.
//
// Test plan:
//   1. With mcounteren.TM=1 and hcounteren.TM=0, every CSR instruction form on stimecmp in VS-mode
//     raises virtual-instruction; on RV32 also stimecmph.
//   2. hcounteren.TM does not gate HS-mode: with hcounteren.TM=0, HS-mode accesses to vstimecmp and
//     stimecmp succeed.
//   3. Negative dependency: hcounteren.TM=0 does not stop the vstimecmp comparison: vstimecmp = 0
//     gives hip.VSTIP=1, all ones gives 0.
//   4. hcounteren.TM=1 permits access: a VS-mode read returns vstimecmp and a write lands in it
//     (or, if hcounteren.TM is read-only zero, the read still raises virtual-instruction).
//   5. mcounteren.TM=0 takes precedence: VS-mode access raises illegal-instruction with
//     hcounteren.TM at 1 and 0.
//
// Approximate dynamic instruction count: 10134
//

